The Fragment gives the function one returned value without inserting a wrapper element into the DOM.

```jsx
function Inbox({ user, unreadMessages }) {
  const unreadCount = unreadMessages.length;

  return (
    <section>
      <h2>{user.name}'s inbox</h2>
      {unreadCount > 0 && <p>{unreadCount} unread</p>}
      {unreadMessages.map(message => (
        <p key={message.id}>{message.subject}</p>
      ))}
    </section>
  );
}
```
When the array is empty, no message paragraphs are created. The explicit greater-than-zero check prevents an unwanted zero text node.

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(2);
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Orders</h1>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>
    </main>
  );
}
```
On a click, React recalculates the component. The heading remains equivalent; the committed change is the button's text.
